* Numbers too big for a cell.
*
* A cell holds 0 to 255, and plenty of programs need more. A wide number here
* is two cells -- `hi` and `lo`, worth hi*256 + lo -- and every macro below
* takes both, so a program says `@wide_dec(n_hi, n_lo, ...)` and never has to
* remember which is which.
*
* This is the interesting thing about the file: multi-cell arithmetic was
* written down as a missing *language* feature, something the expander would
* have to grow. It is not. It is a library, and a short one, because a
* parameter can name a cell and macros compose.
*
* Every carry and borrow below is the same idiom, the one BrainFuck uses for
* every branch: empty a cell into a loop, so the loop runs once and stops.
*
* Each macro carries what it would be in a language with two-byte integers,
* which is the shortest way to say what the loops underneath it are for.

@include "idioms.bfm"

* Both halves zero.
*
*     hi, lo = 0, 0
@macro wide_clear(hi, lo) {
    @clear(hi)
    @clear(lo)
}

* A wide number holding hi*256 + lo.
*
*     hi, lo = high, low
@macro wide_put(hi, lo, high, low) {
    @put(hi, high)
    @put(lo, low)
}

* One less. If the low half is not zero it goes down by one; if it is, it
* wraps to 255 and the high half goes down instead.
*
* `held` is where the low half is parked so that the loop reading it ends;
* `flag` says whether the borrow is still owed. Both are left empty.
*
*     if lo:
*         lo -= 1
*     else:
*         lo = 255
*         hi -= 1
@macro wide_dec(hi, lo, flag, held) {
    @put(flag, 1)
    @to lo
    [
        -
        @clear(flag)
        @move(lo, held)
    ]
    @move(held, lo)
    @to flag
    [
        @put(lo, 255)
        @to hi
        -
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* One more. The carry is read off the wrap rather than predicted: add one, and
* if the low half is now zero it must have gone past 255.
*
*     lo += 1
*     if lo == 0:              # it went past 255 and came back
*         hi += 1
@macro wide_inc(hi, lo, flag, held) {
    @to lo
    +
    @put(flag, 1)
    @to lo
    [
        @clear(flag)
        @move(lo, held)
    ]
    @move(held, lo)
    @to flag
    [
        @to hi
        +
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* `flag` becomes 1 if the number is zero, 0 if it is not.
*
*     flag = not hi and not lo
@macro wide_is_zero(hi, lo, flag, scratch, spare) {
    @put(flag, 1)
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(hi, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @clear(flag)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(lo, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @clear(flag)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
}

* And the other way round, which is what a loop wants to test.
*
*     flag = bool(hi or lo)
@macro wide_is_not_zero(hi, lo, flag, scratch, spare) {
    @clear(flag)
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(hi, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @put(flag, 1)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(lo, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @put(flag, 1)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
}

* `a` gains what `b` has; `b` keeps it.
*
*     a_hi += b_hi
*     for _ in range(b_lo):
*         a += 1                # carrying into the high half as it wraps
*
* The halves separately, which is how every machine with bytes does it and is
* the whole reason to hold a number this way. Adding `b` by counting it down
* one at a time would be right too, and would cost up to 65,535 steps where
* this costs at most 510.
@macro wide_add(a_hi, a_lo, b_hi, b_lo, count, flag, held, spare) {
    @copy(b_hi, a_hi, spare)
    @clear(count)
    @copy(b_lo, count, spare)
    @to count
    [
        @wide_inc(a_hi, a_lo, flag, held)
        @to count
        -
    ]
}

* `a` loses what `b` has; `b` keeps it. Going below zero wraps, as a cell does.
*
*     a_hi -= b_hi
*     for _ in range(b_lo):
*         a -= 1                # borrowing from the high half as it wraps
@macro wide_sub(a_hi, a_lo, b_hi, b_lo, count, flag, held, spare) {
    @clear(count)
    @copy(b_hi, count, spare)
    @to count
    [
        @to a_hi
        -
        @to count
        -
    ]
    @clear(count)
    @copy(b_lo, count, spare)
    @to count
    [
        @wide_dec(a_hi, a_lo, flag, held)
        @to count
        -
    ]
}

* `result` becomes 1 if `a` is less than `b`, and 0 if it is not; both keep
* their values.
*
*     if a_hi != b_hi:
*         result = a_hi < b_hi
*     else:
*         result = a_lo < b_lo
*
* The high halves decide it unless they agree, which is the whole of comparing
* two numbers written as digits.
@macro wide_less(a_hi, a_lo, b_hi, b_lo, result, same, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare) {
    @less(a_hi, b_hi, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
    @clear(same)
    @copy(a_hi, same, spare)
    @equal(same, b_hi, scratch, spare)
    @to same
    [
        @less(a_lo, b_lo, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
        @clear(same)
    ]
}

* `a` becomes `a` times `b`; `b` keeps its value.
*
*     result = 0
*     for _ in range(a):        # a is two cells, so the counting is nested
*         result += b
*     a = result
*
* Adding `b` to nothing, `a` times over -- which is what multiplication is
* when the only arithmetic is adding one, and which costs the *value* of `a`
* rather than the number of digits in it. That is fine for checking an answer
* and much too slow for a program that does it in a loop; a version that
* doubles and adds costs the digits instead.
*
* I wrote that version and measured it, and for `mandelbrot.bfm` it is 2.5
* times *slower* -- 227,244,885 steps against 91,513,283 -- because its eight
* rounds happen whatever the numbers are, a floor of about 9,500 steps a
* multiply, and mandelbrot multiplies small numbers and zeros. It is the right
* shape for large operands and the wrong one here, so it was reverted;
* `docs/performance.md` has the numbers.
*
* The counting is nested because a counter of two cells cannot be tested for
* zero cheaply: the low half runs 256 times for each step of the high half,
* which is the same shape a machine uses to loop more times than a register
* can hold.
@macro wide_multiply(a_hi, a_lo, b_hi, b_lo, r_hi, r_lo, c_hi, c_lo, count, flag, held, spare) {
    @wide_clear(r_hi, r_lo)
    @wide_copy(a_hi, a_lo, c_hi, c_lo, spare)

    @to c_hi
    [
        @to c_lo
        [
            @wide_add(r_hi, r_lo, b_hi, b_lo, count, flag, held, spare)
            @to c_lo
            -
        ]
        * The step that takes the low half from zero to 255 and the high half
        * down by one: 256 of them to an outer turn, and this is the 256th.
        @wide_add(r_hi, r_lo, b_hi, b_lo, count, flag, held, spare)
        @put(c_lo, 255)
        @to c_hi
        -
    ]
    @to c_lo
    [
        @wide_add(r_hi, r_lo, b_hi, b_lo, count, flag, held, spare)
        @to c_lo
        -
    ]

    @wide_clear(a_hi, a_lo)
    @wide_copy(r_hi, r_lo, a_hi, a_lo, spare)
}

* `to` gains what `from` has, and `from` keeps it.
*
*     to_hi, to_lo = from_hi, from_lo
@macro wide_copy(from_hi, from_lo, to_hi, to_lo, scratch) {
    @clear(to_hi)
    @clear(to_lo)
    @copy(from_hi, to_hi, scratch)
    @copy(from_lo, to_lo, scratch)
}
